# Cloudflare Pages redirects. First match wins; 200 = rewrite, 301/302 = redirect.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND CANNOT BE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The legacy site was a single index.html and every address it was indexed
# under is a fragment: exclamationdev.com/#crm, /#services, /#faq. A fragment
# is never sent to the server — the browser strips it before the request and
# resolves it locally — so Cloudflare cannot see, match or redirect one. No
# rule here can move /#crm to the CRM Portal case page.
#
# Those are handled in the browser instead, by LegacyHash.astro on the three
# landing pages, which rewrites a known legacy fragment to its new home on
# first paint. That file is the companion to this one; change both together.
#
# The redirects below are for the addresses that ARE sent to the server.

# WWW IS NOT HANDLED HERE — IT CANNOT BE.
#
# This file used to carry
#
#   https://www.exclamationdev.com/* https://exclamationdev.com/:splat 301!
#
# and that rule never fired. Cloudflare Pages matches `_redirects` sources on
# the path alone; a source with a hostname in it matches no request at all. The
# symptom was silent — `curl -I https://www.exclamationdev.com/` answered 200
# with a full copy of the site rather than a redirect, checked 2026-08-14.
#
# The 301 lives in `functions/_middleware.ts` instead, which can see the Host
# header. Same reasoning as the fragments above: if the server cannot match on
# it, the rule does not belong in this file.

# The legacy site's own files. They are still in the repository and still
# served by GitHub Pages until the cutover; once Cloudflare is authoritative
# these paths should land somewhere real rather than 404.
/index.html / 301
/style_dark.css / 301
/style_light.css / 301
/translations.js / 301
/pics/* / 301

# Trailing-slash-less variants of the localised roots. Cloudflare 308s these
# on its own, but being explicit costs nothing and documents the intent.
/ru /ru/ 301
/en /en/ 301
